\section{Error Analysis \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:analysis}

The following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from LLM-coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

We analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes ($\kappa = 0.52$ \citep{cohen_1960}; Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$ \citep{gwet_2014}); the coder prompt excludes ambiguity type labels. Type--mechanism mutual information (NMI $= 0.23$; $V = 0.45$, 95\% CI $[0.41, 0.50]$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}) provides structural evidence against LLM-coding circularity.\footnote{$V$ is computed on a $6\times 5$ table vs.\ $6\times 2$ for severity; higher dimensionality makes $V = 0.45$ conservative.} Human mechanism coding yields $\kappa = 0.32$, rising to $0.41$ after collapsing the spurious-compliance/structural-misparse boundary; human-coded $V = 0.40$ (mean of two annotators; collapsed $V = 0.42$; Appendix~\ref{app:human_validation}) corroborates the LLM-coded association ($V = 0.45$), which remains a moderate upper bound. The higher LLM agreement ($\kappa = 0.52$) than human ($0.32$) may reflect shared classification conventions---a meta-level parallel to convergent nocuity (\S\ref{sec:nocuity})---so these patterns are best interpreted as categorization tendencies.

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}

Three failure patterns emerge with $\geq$3/5 cross-model consistency (Figure~\ref{fig:type_mechanism}; Appendix~\ref{app:failure_details}): \emph{structural misparse} dominates scopal, conditional precedence, and coreferential types; \emph{information gap-filling} dominates incompleteness and lexical; and \emph{boundary violation} dominates authorization scope exclusively (69.2\%, $p < 10^{-4}$).

\subsection{Disambiguation Neglect}
\label{sec:dn}

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity: overall awareness is 4.6\%, with GPT-4.1 and Claude at 10--11\% while Qwen3 and DeepSeek show zero ($p = 2.2 \times 10^{-9}$; Appendix~\ref{app:awareness_breakdown}). We term this \emph{disambiguation neglect}---extending ambiguity collapse \citep{liu_ambiguity_2023,hu_ambiguity_2025} from the representational level to the behavioral level: agents act on collapsed readings without recognizing ambiguity, producing irreversible safety violations rather than mere accuracy degradation. Convergent nocuity (\S\ref{sec:nocuity}) further extends to the population level. A prompt-based mitigation (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}) confirms type-dependent effectiveness: authorization scope ($-$18.0\,pp), lexical ($-$8.9\,pp), but not incompleteness ($-$3.4\,pp, n.s.).

\subsection{Convergent Nocuity}
\label{sec:nocuity}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_convergent_nocuity.pdf}
\caption{Convergent nocuity. (a)~Among majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models), 77\% reflect permissive interpretation; authorization scope shows near-universal permissive bias (99\%), while conditional precedence splits between permissive and restrictive. (b)~Pairwise Jaccard similarity of violation clause sets: cross-family mean (0.46) approaches within-family levels (0.54; dashed box), indicating convergence reflects shared architectural biases rather than training-data overlap.}
\label{fig:convergent_nocuity}
\end{figure}

We adopt `nocuous' from \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}, but inverted: our agents exhibit \emph{convergent} rather than \emph{divergent} errors---whereas divergent nocuity produces detectable disagreement, convergent nocuity renders errors invisible to agreement-based monitoring. Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models; 13.0\% unanimous)---we term this \emph{convergent nocuity}, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. Under an independence assumption, the expected $\geq$3/5 convergence rate is 32.4\%; the observed 41.3\% exceeds this by $\sim$9\,pp ($p < 0.0001$; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_family}). These convergent violations exhibit a \emph{permissive attractor}: 77.0\% reflect permissive interpretation ($p \approx 0$; Appendix~\ref{app:attractor}). Cross-family Jaccard ($0.46$; Figure~\ref{fig:convergent_nocuity}) approaches within-family levels ($0.54$; $\Delta = 0.084$ [0.016, 0.153]), indicating shared architectural biases rather than training-data overlap. The ambient convergent violation rate under unambiguous conditions is 1.7\% (5/300 clauses), confirming a $+$39.6\,pp ambiguity-induced excess. Excluding incompleteness, the excess over the independence baseline remains robust ($+$8.3\,pp, 95\% CI $[2.2, 14.3]$, $p < 0.005$; Appendix~\ref{app:nocuity_sans_incomp}).

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Experimental Platform}

We conduct all experiments on the original release of $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark spanning retail and airline customer service domains. $\tau^2$-bench provides natural-language policy documents, tool APIs, and multi-turn user interactions with ground-truth evaluation via assertion checking. Its policy injection mechanism allows us to replace individual policy clauses while preserving the surrounding context, enabling controlled ambiguity manipulation.

\subsection{Matched-Pair Design}

For each of the six ambiguity types, we construct 50 \emph{clause pairs}: an ambiguous variant and a matched unambiguous variant that conveys the same intended policy but resolves the ambiguity. Pairs are minimal edits: the unambiguous variant adds only the information necessary to eliminate the target ambiguity (e.g., for scopal ambiguity, replacing ``items may be returned within 30 days'' with ``standard items may be returned within 30 days; customized items are final sale''). This yields 300 clause pairs $\times$ 2 conditions $\times$ 5 models $= 2{,}997$ episodes (3 episodes lost to API errors in one Qwen3 batch). Appendix~\ref{app:pipeline} details the construction pipeline; Appendix~\ref{app:clause_examples} provides representative examples for each type.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{1.4cm}p{3.0cm}p{3.0cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Type} & \textbf{\textcolor{speccolor}{Ambiguous}} & \textbf{\textcolor{lingcolor}{Disambiguated}} \\
\midrule
\raggedright Incomp. \scriptsize{(Spec.)} & \scriptsize Refunds for \textbf{large orders} require \textbf{appropriate approval} and must be processed \textbf{in a timely manner}. & \scriptsize Refunds for orders over \$50 require manager approval and must be processed within 14 business days. \\
\addlinespace[2pt]
\raggedright Scopal \scriptsize{(Ling.)} & \scriptsize \textbf{All items} are eligible for return except restricted categories. & \scriptsize All items in the order are eligible for return \textbf{except final-sale items and personalized products}. \\
\addlinespace[2pt]
\raggedright Auth.\ Scope \scriptsize{(Spec.)} & \scriptsize The agent may issue refunds \textbf{autonomously for routine amounts}; larger amounts require escalation. & \scriptsize The agent may issue refunds \textbf{up to \$50} without supervisor approval; amounts above \$50 require sign-off. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Representative clause pairs. Ambiguous variants introduce a single ambiguity type; disambiguated variants resolve it through minimal edits (bold highlights key differences). Full set: Appendix~\ref{app:clause_examples}.}
\label{fig:clause_examples}
\end{figure}

Clause pairs are hand-crafted following structured type-level specifications and reviewed by two independent annotators (both with NLP background) to ensure (1) the ambiguous variant contains exactly one instance of the target type, (2) the unambiguous variant resolves it without changing policy scope, and (3) the ambiguity is plausibly natural. Annotators independently verified type assignments with a 96\% initial agreement (287/300; Cohen's $\kappa = 0.95$ on six-type classification); this high agreement reflects annotation precision---each pair differs only in the ambiguity-introducing edit---not task simplicity. The 13 disagreements were resolved by discussion, yielding 100\% final consensus. A post-hoc realism validation confirms linguistic consistency with original $\tau^2$-bench policy text (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

\subsection{Models}

We evaluate five models from four providers spanning two architecture families: GPT-5.4 and GPT-4.1 (OpenAI, proprietary dense), Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic, proprietary dense), Qwen3-235B (Alibaba, open-source Mixture-of-Experts), and DeepSeek-V3 (DeepSeek, open-source dense). This selection covers frontier and mid-tier proprietary models alongside open-source alternatives with different training philosophies. All API calls use temperature $= 0$, top\_p $= 1$, and max\_tokens $= 4096$, and were made on May 20, 2026.

\subsection{Evaluation}

We use a cross-judge design to mitigate self-evaluation bias: GPT-5.4 judges Claude, GPT-4.1, and Qwen3 traces; GPT-4.1 judges GPT-5.4 and DeepSeek traces. Because our matched-pair design compares conditions within the same model--judge pairing, systematic judge bias cancels in the treatment effect ($\Delta$pp); cross-model judge asymmetry affects only absolute rates, not C1 or C2 (\S\ref{sec:limitations}). Judges classify episodes on a four-tier severity scale (none, minor, moderate, critical); our primary threshold is moderate-or-above (Appendix~\ref{app:severity}). Dual-judge calibration on 100 stratified episodes yields $\kappa = 0.653$ ($\kappa_w = 0.775$; per-type $n \approx 17$, CIs wide; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_agreement}). Three-family validation (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Claude; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_judge}) yields Fleiss' $\kappa = 0.717$. The judge prompt template is in Appendix~\ref{app:judge_prompt}.

\paragraph{Human validation.} Two annotators, blind to LLM labels, labeled 100 stratified episodes. Inter-annotator $\kappa = 0.798$; human--LLM $\kappa = 0.733$ ($\kappa_w = 0.807$), approaching inter-annotator levels and validating the automated pipeline \citep{zheng_llm_judge_2024}. Per-type estimates ($n \approx 32$--$34$) have wide CIs (Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_agreement}).

\subsection{Controls}

\paragraph{Perturbation control.} To confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity, we run 300 episodes with non-ambiguity perturbations (synonym substitution, clause reordering). The perturbation effect is $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$ pp (95\% CI $[-8.0, 6.67]$, containing zero). Even under the worst-case CI bound (6.67 pp), the residual attributable to ambiguity is $\geq$27.3 pp. This control tests surface-level perturbation specificity; the information-addition confound specific to incompleteness is addressed separately via logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}).

\paragraph{Within-family replicability.} A replication of 30 clause pairs $\times$ 2 models $\times$ 3 replicates yields an intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) of $0.919$ (95\% CI $[0.892, 0.940]$); cross-provider concordance is moderate (Kendall's $W = 0.398$, $p = 0.072$; \S\ref{sec:results}).

\subsection{Statistical Analysis}

We use McNemar's test for paired binary outcomes, $\chi^2$ tests with Cram\'{e}r's $V$ for omnibus effects, Fisher's exact test for per-type comparisons, and two one-sided tests (TOST) equivalence testing \citep{lakens_2017} with a primary smallest effect size of interest (SESOI) of $\pm 15$ pp. We report the matched-pair odds ratio (OR) from McNemar's test on discordant outcomes ($n_{\text{disc}} = 580$ of 1{,}497 pairs), which conditions on within-pair disagreement and thus differs from the crude marginal odds ratio. No industry-standard SESOI exists for LLM agent policy violations. We use $\pm 15$\,pp as a pragmatic threshold: it exceeds measurement noise (perturbation control upper CI: 6.67\,pp) by $>$2$\times$, and a per-type difference below this margin would not warrant distinct mitigation strategies. This is a pragmatic default, not a principled domain-anchored threshold (see Appendix~\ref{app:tost} for cost anchoring); Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity} reports sensitivity across seven margins ($\pm 5$--$20$\,pp; \citealp{anvari_lakens_2021}). The margin-free bootstrap cluster analysis (\S\ref{sec:results}; Appendix~\ref{app:cluster}) confirms the same equivalence structure. We apply Holm--Bonferroni correction within three pre-registered test families (Appendix~\ref{app:multiple_comparison}); post-hoc power exceeds 0.99 for C1 and C2.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around three analyses: the binary ambiguity effect (C1), the type partial ordering (C2, including the inconclusive layer effect C3), and the model$\times$type interaction (C4). C1 and C2 are primary supported claims; C3 (layer effect) is inconclusive; C4 (model$\times$type interaction) is exploratory.

\subsection{Binary Ambiguity Effect (C1)}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_variance_decomposition.pdf}
\caption{Variance decomposition of violation rates (temperature $= 0$). Clause identity: 22.4\% (0.4\% between types); condition: 15.8\%; model: 1.0\%; residual: 60.8\%. Clause wording---not type membership---is the primary risk factor among measured predictors.}
\label{fig:variance_decomposition}
\end{figure}

Across all five models and six types, ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 pp (41.0\% vs.\ 6.9\%, McNemar OR $= 15.1$ (based on $n = 580$ discordant pairs), 95\% CI $[10.78, 21.17]$, $p < 10^{-105}$; stratified Mantel--Haenszel OR $= 9.57$ $[7.63, 12.01]$; Appendix~\ref{app:mantel_haenszel}). All six types individually produce significant increases (all $p < 10^{-5}$) across both retail ($+$30.6\,pp) and airline ($+$37.7\,pp) domains (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_domain}), and all five models show large effects ($+25.7$ to $+39.0$ pp, all $p < 10^{-17}$). Perturbation controls confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity (\S\ref{sec:method}). Figure~\ref{fig:variance_decomposition} decomposes violation-rate variance\footnote{Type III sum of squares (SS); logistic regression (Appendix~\ref{app:regression}) confirms concordant results (Tjur's $R^2 = 0.175$; \citealp{tjur_2009}).}: clause identity explains 22.4\% (0.4\% between types, 22.0\% within), condition 15.8\%, model 1.0\%, and residual 60.8\% ($\sim$15\% stochastic, $\sim$46\% systematic; Appendix~\ref{app:residual}). Excluding incompleteness ($+$49.2\,pp, subject to information-addition confound; Appendix~\ref{app:confound}), the effect is $+$31.0\,pp (95\% CI $[27.9, 34.1]$; Appendix~\ref{app:sans_incompleteness}). The confound-minimized coreferential type ($\Delta$token $\approx 7.8$ words) still produces $+$18.7\,pp, isolating the ambiguity-only contribution. $\Delta_{\text{token}}$ is a coarse proxy for information content; authorization scope exhibits similar token differences ($\Delta = 36.1$ words) without the information-addition confound. A logistic regression controlling for per-clause token-count differences confirms the effect persists at $\Delta_{\text{token}} = 0$ ($+$20.5\,pp, 95\% CI $[14.2, 26.8]$; Appendix~\ref{app:token_sensitivity}).

Convergent evidence from two additional models confirms the effect across seven models ($\Delta = +20.0$ to $+33.6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}); cross-benchmark evaluation on SOPBench~\citep{li_sopbench_2025} shows directional consistency ($+12.7$\,pp unweighted mean; Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}). The taxonomy predicts failure mechanisms more strongly than violation rate (NMI $= 0.23$ vs.\ $0.01$; $V = 0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$ on tables of different dimensionality; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}), making type-level analysis (C2--C4) informative for diagnosis.

\FloatBarrier
\subsection{Type Partial Ordering (C2)}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Violation rates (\%) under ambiguous policy clauses by type and model (5.4 = GPT-5.4, 4.1 = GPT-4.1, Cla.\ = Claude, Qw3 = Qwen3, DS = DeepSeek). $\Delta$pp: pooled difference (ambiguous $-$ unambiguous) with Newcombe 95\% CIs. Cluster: TOST equivalence cluster at $\pm 15$\,pp---U = upper bound (separated at all margins), M = mid-tier (6/6 pairwise equivalent), L = lower (separates at $\pm 12$\,pp). Layer grouping (Spec./Ling.) did not survive correction (C3, inconclusive; \S\ref{sec:results}). All per-type $p < 10^{-5}$ (Fisher's exact).}
\label{tab:main_results}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.2pt}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrrr@{\hskip 6pt}cr}
\toprule
Layer & Type & Pooled & 5.4 & 4.1 & Cla. & Qw3 & DS & $\Delta$pp\,[95\% CI] & Cl. & $p$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\textcolor{speccolor}{Spec.}} & Incompleteness$^\dagger$ & 53.6 & 46 & 58 & 48 & 62 & 54 & +49.2\,[42.2, 55.6] & U & $<\!10^{-37}$ \\
 & Auth.\ Scope$^\ddagger$ & 41.6 & 64 & 46 & 12 & 26 & 60 & +36.0\,[29.1, 42.6] & M & $<\!10^{-22}$ \\
 & Cond.\ Prec. & 36.8 & 34 & 36 & 36 & 40 & 38 & +30.8\,[24.0, 37.4] & M & $<\!10^{-17}$ \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\textcolor{lingcolor}{Ling.}} & Lexical & 42.8 & 42 & 34 & 32 & 52 & 54 & +35.2\,[28.0, 42.0] & M & $<\!10^{-20}$ \\
 & Scopal & 39.4 & 42 & 38 & 32 & 37 & 48 & +34.2\,[27.4, 40.7] & M & $<\!10^{-21}$ \\
 & Coreferential & 31.6 & 18 & 32 & 22 & 42 & 44 & +18.7\,[11.5, 25.7] & L & $<\!10^{-6}$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{\textbf{Overall}} & \textbf{41.0} & 41 & 41 & 30 & 43 & 50 & \textbf{+34.0}\,[31.3, 36.9] & --- & $<\!10^{-105}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\vspace{1pt}
{\scriptsize $\dagger$\,Information-addition confound (\S\ref{sec:results}; Appendix~\ref{app:confound}). $\ddagger$\,Lowest cross-judge $\kappa = 0.385$ (dual-judge calibration, $n \approx 17$); $\Delta$token$\,= 36.1$ words, analogous confound. Excl.\ incompleteness: $\Delta = +31.0$\,pp [27.9, 34.1].}
\end{table*}

The six types are not interchangeable. An omnibus $\chi^2$ test on ambiguous-condition violation rates rejects the uniformity null ($\chi^2 = 28.02$, $df = 5$, $p = 3.6 \times 10^{-5}$, Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.137$). Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity} reveals a margin-dependent equivalence structure: three types achieve equivalence at $\pm 10$\,pp, five at $\pm 12$\,pp, and all six at $\pm 15$\,pp. The resulting partial ordering is exploratory and contingent on the $\pm 15$\,pp SESOI choice (Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity} sensitivity columns show instability below $\pm 12$\,pp). The NMI comparison ($0.23$ vs.\ $0.01$) is threshold-independent.

Bootstrap clustering ($B = 10{,}000$; Appendix~\ref{app:cluster}) confirms stable extremes across all margins. Cross-model concordance is moderate ($W = 0.398$, $p = 0.072$; Appendix~\ref{app:kendall_w}). Per-type inter-judge agreement ranges from $\kappa = 0.479$ (coreferential) to $0.904$ (lexical; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_agreement}). For coreferential, whose per-type $\kappa$ falls below the moderate threshold ($n = 34$), per-type effect estimates should be interpreted with caution; in matched-pair designs, random measurement error attenuates effects toward zero, making these estimates conservative. Authorization scope exhibits the widest cross-model range (12\%--64\%) and the lowest per-type judge agreement; conclusions about this type carry wider uncertainty than the pooled estimates.

Cross-model judge validation ($\rho = 0.71$--$0.89$; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_judge}) confirms type-ranking stability when model composition is held constant; the low two-group concordance ($\rho = 0.09$; Appendix~\ref{app:judge_group_ranking}) reflects differential vulnerability to authorization scope$^\ddagger$ (12\%--64\% range), not ranking instability. The partial ordering has robust extremes---incompleteness as upper bound and coreferential as lower bound, replicated across seven models and three judges---with model-composition-dependent mid-tier structure.

Incompleteness ranks highest in four of five models ($\geq$46\% in all seven; Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}); its 49.2\,pp effect is an upper bound (Appendix~\ref{app:confound}). C3 (layer grouping) did not survive Holm--Bonferroni ($p = 0.017$, threshold $0.0125$).

\begin{figure}[!ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_forest_plot.pdf}
\caption{Per-type effect sizes ($\Delta$pp) with Newcombe 95\% CIs (overall: $+$34.0\,pp, OR $= 15.1$). Types form a partial ordering: incompleteness as upper bound (conditional on information-addition confound), coreferential as lower bound, and a mid-range equivalence cluster. Mid-tier ordering is margin-dependent (\S\ref{sec:results}).}
\label{fig:forest_plot}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Violation rates (\%) by model and type under ambiguous conditions. Authorization scope varies 52\,pp across models ($\kappa = 0.385$), reflecting differential safety training; incompleteness remains uniformly high (46--62\%).}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

\label{sec:c4}C4 (model$\times$type interaction) is exploratory and driven solely by authorization scope; details in Appendix~\ref{app:c4_sans_auth}.

